  "slug": "nf-pipeline-e163",
  "summary": "Molecular dynamics and enhanced sampling now link mechanical coupling in nucleic acid machines to specific functional outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the dominant thread is physics-based modeling of how nucleic acid assemblies convert conformational or mechanical states into function. A preprint proposes cohesin bridging during loop extrusion as a physical route for enhancer-promoter communication, with a quantitative model of transcriptional output. Molecular dynamics studies of U2 snRNP propose a loaded-spring mechanism in which TAT-SF1 latching of the branch-stem loop, upon release, drives toehold-mediated strand invasion. Enhanced-sampling simulations map free energy landscapes of MutY lesion recognition, identifying a linker-coordinated pathway. A mechanics-aware RNA design framework with topology constraints and MD-guided optimization yields functional de novo xrRNAs that stall XRN1. The new element is the use of mechanical and free-energy considerations to predict or engineer functional behavior, rather than only to describe structure.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-05-11"
}
